{Complete standard game against the site's level-3 computer, anonymous, casual, unlimited time. No analysis panel, external engine, hints, or takebacks used during play. Final visible UI verified 52.Qa4#, 1-0, White victory. Opponent made substantial mistakes; this result alone is not a measure of playing strength.}
1. e4 c6 2. d4 d5 3. Nc3 c5 4. exd5 cxd4 5. Qxd4 Nf6
6. Nf3 e6 7. Bb5+ Bd7 8. O-O Nxd5 9. Bxd7+ Qxd7
10. Nxd5 Qxd5 11. Qxd5 exd5 12. Re1+ Kd7
13. Bf4 Nc6 14. Rad1 Rd8 15. Rxd5+ Bd6 16. Bxd6 h6
17. Bc7+ {I miscounted the resulting exchanges when choosing this discovered check. After Kxc7 and the rook exchange I retain only the extra pawn, losing the extra bishop I had just gained. This is my own observation during play, not an engine evaluation.} Kxc7
18. Rxd8 Kxd8 19. Kf1 g5 20. c3 Kc8 21. Nd4 Rd8
22. Nxc6 Rf8 {Black did not recapture the knight with the b7 pawn.}
23. Nd4 Rg8 24. Re7 Rd8 25. Rxf7 g4 26. Ke2 a5 27. Kd3 a4 28. Rf6 h5 29. Rh6 Rd5 30. Ke4 Rd8
31. Rxh5 Rd6 32. Rg5 Rb6 33. b3 Rf6 34. Rf5 Rh6
35. Kf4 Rh8 36. Kxg4 axb3 37. axb3 Rxh2 38. Kg3 Rh8
39. f4 Re8 40. Re5 Rd8 41. f5 b6 42. Re7 Rg8+
43. Kf4 Rd8 44. f6 Rxd4+ 45. cxd4 Kd8
46. Re5 {My observation during play: moving the f6 pawn immediately would remove its protection of Re7 against the black king on d8. Re5 puts the rook under my king's protection before advancing the pawn.} Kd7
47. f7 Kc6 48. f8=Q Kc7 49. Qf6 Kb7 50. Re7+ Ka6
51. Qc6 Ka5 52. Qa4# {The b3 pawn protects the queen.} 1-0
